WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.241 --> 00:00:10.443
Hi, I'm Art Horwich, from Yale School of Medicine and HHMI.

00:00:10.444 --> 00:00:17.984
And I want to tell you a story about a discovery that has led to a really wonderful

00:00:17.985 --> 00:00:23.656
what's now a fifteen to twenty year experience of experimentation that followed.

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:27.326
This discovery was really somewhat serendipitous,

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:29.328
and I think there was a lot of luck involved.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:36.335
It was helped enormously by two wonderful collaborators, who have remained very close friends,

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:41.440
and constant contacts, Ming Cheng, who's now in Taiwan,

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:44.477
and Ulrich Hartl, who's now in Martinsried.

00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:48.347
And so I'll just tell you the story as it occurred.

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:52.585
So, the story is about information transfer in the cell.

00:00:52.586 --> 00:00:57.456
As we know, DNA is transcribed into RNA and is in turn translated into protein

00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:03.262
and the step that's involved here is the final step of information transfer,

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:06.032
namely that of protein folding.

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:10.169
So, we know about machines that carry out transcription and translation,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:16.742
and have very fine molecular details to those reactions for some years now,

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.378
increasingly fine.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:23.482
But protein folding has remained somewhat mysterious

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.618
and it's really only during the last decade or two

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:31.791
that we've really started to gain some understanding about how this works in cells.

00:01:31.792 --> 00:01:38.598
So, for those unfamiliar, I'd just point out that proteins are composed of twenty different amino acids,

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:44.637
and these strings of amino acids ultimately fold into characteristic three-dimensional structures

00:01:44.638 --> 00:01:47.139
that carry out biological activities.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:52.645
For example, they can be enzymes, cytoskeleton, hormones, hemoglobin, channels,

00:01:52.646 --> 00:01:54.547
receptors, what have you.

00:01:54.548 --> 00:01:57.750
Proteins are major effectors in the cell

00:01:57.751 --> 00:02:03.256
and so their proper folding is crucial to them being biologically active,

00:02:03.257 --> 00:02:06.993
because when they're effectively strands of spaghetti,

00:02:06.994 --> 00:02:13.266
leaving a ribosome, they don't necessarily have any biological activity at that point.

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:16.802
They have to be folded correctly in order to be active.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:20.006
And so the granddaddy experiment of protein folding

00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:24.143
that I heard about when I was an undergraduate at Brown University

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:30.983
in 1972 was carried out by Christian Anfinsen, in fact, in the late 50's,

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:35.922
he received his Nobel Prize for this work in 1972,

00:02:35.923 --> 00:02:39.792
a really astonishing experiment where he started with an enzyme,

00:02:39.793 --> 00:02:44.197
ribonuclease, 100-odd amino acids with 4 disulfide bonds,

00:02:44.198 --> 00:02:48.701
and unfolded that protein by treating it with denaturant,  urea,

00:02:48.702 --> 00:02:50.970
and reductant, mercaptoethanol.

00:02:50.971 --> 00:02:54.907
That produced essentially a random coil version of the protein,

00:02:54.908 --> 00:03:00.446
and he asked whether, when he removed the urea and reductant,

00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:04.684
whether the protein could find its way back, on its on, in a test tube,

00:03:04.685 --> 00:03:09.021
to the native active form, and astonishingly, it did so.

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:14.393
So, this proved that a polypeptide chain contains all of the information

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:20.700
for folding into the unique native structure in its primary amino acid sequence structure.

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:27.573
He also presumed that in fact this native state would lie at an energetic minimum.

00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:36.649
And so, this astonishing experiment led people to believe that perhaps inside cells,

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.452
proteins also fold spontaneously.

00:03:39.453 --> 00:03:44.123
But increasingly, there were instances where that didn't really happen,

00:03:44.124 --> 00:03:50.196
both in test tube, where many proteins could also not be spontaneously refolded,

00:03:50.197 --> 00:03:53.633
as in the Anfinsen experiment, with ribonuclease,

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:58.971
but inside cells when the biotech industry began to express mammalian proteins in E. coli,

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:04.877
for example, they also saw these terrible looking aggregates with no biological activity

00:04:04.878 --> 00:04:09.415
and failed to recover their favorite proteins in a native, active form.

00:04:09.416 --> 00:04:13.853
And this, once again, one saw, as in the test tube,

00:04:13.854 --> 00:04:18.691
a misfolded, aggregated, inclusion body, as it were.

00:04:18.692 --> 00:04:23.796
And so this suggested that something more might be required in the cell

00:04:23.797 --> 00:04:30.336
to deal with these so-called, what would be kinetic problems of folding,

00:04:30.337 --> 00:04:34.240
where proteins simply get it wrong and go off pathway

00:04:34.241 --> 00:04:36.008
and misfold and aggregate.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:40.613
And so we never thought we would have anything to do with this,

00:04:40.614 --> 00:04:48.654
but we were busy in the late 80's studying mitochondrial protein import.

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:54.760
So, it turns out that most of the proteins of mitochondria are encoded in the nuclear genome,

00:04:54.761 --> 00:05:02.535
the messages for these proteins are then translated on cytosolic polyribosomes

00:05:02.536 --> 00:05:10.176
and an N-terminal targeting peptide in these proteins directs them to mitochondria.

00:05:10.177 --> 00:05:14.514
And they're then imported through the mitochondrial membranes,

00:05:14.515 --> 00:05:18.985
and the targeting peptide is then clipped and the mature-sized protein

00:05:18.986 --> 00:05:22.955
then has to fold into its biologically active form.

00:05:22.956 --> 00:05:28.594
Well, an important experiment was carried out in 1986 by Gottfried Schatz and his coworkers

00:05:28.595 --> 00:05:35.635
that showed that a protein had to be unfolded in order to transit the mitochondrial membranes.

00:05:35.636 --> 00:05:41.107
He used dihydrofolate reductase as a substrate in these experiments,

00:05:41.108 --> 00:05:44.744
showing that if you lock DHFR into its native state,

00:05:44.745 --> 00:05:49.649
by adding the ligand methotrexate, the protein couldn't go through the membranes,

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:54.220
whereas, if you now removed methotrexate or denatured the protein,

00:05:54.221 --> 00:05:55.855
it would go through the membranes.

00:05:55.856 --> 00:06:01.127
So, the question that arose to us, as we were studying mitochondrial protein import,

00:06:01.128 --> 00:06:07.066
was, do proteins, upon reaching the mitochondrial matrix space,

00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:13.372
refold spontaneously to their active form, as might be suggested by the Anfinsen experiments,

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:20.880
or could there be a machinery that was required to assist the protein to reach its native, active form.

00:06:20.881 --> 00:06:24.350
And so we were in a position to actually test this

00:06:24.351 --> 00:06:28.921
because we had a bank of temperature sensitive lethal yeast mutants

00:06:28.922 --> 00:06:34.660
that contained a galactose regulated version of a mitochondrial protein,

00:06:34.661 --> 00:06:39.332
ornithine transcarbamalase, a human protein that we had shown in yeast already

00:06:39.333 --> 00:06:44.403
could be expressed and would go faithfully to its mitochondria, have its signal peptide cleaved,

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:48.174
and become biologically active and make OTC enzymatic activity.

00:06:48.175 --> 00:06:54.380
And so our screen was basically to start with temperature sensitive lethal yeast mutants,

00:06:54.381 --> 00:06:57.116
the idea being from Schatz and others in the field

00:06:57.117 --> 00:07:02.288
that if you can't make new mitochondrial proteins and new mitochondria,

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:07.260
the cell basically stops its growth and ultimately, it's a lethal.

00:07:07.261 --> 00:07:10.363
So, we start with a bank of lethal mutants,

00:07:10.364 --> 00:07:16.068
programmed to express mitochondrial OTC behind a gal operon promoter,

00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:21.908
and we proceeded to test each mutant individually for the ability to make OTC activity

00:07:21.909 --> 00:07:26.145
after shift to non-permissive temperature and shift into galactose,

00:07:26.146 --> 00:07:30.416
to turn on OTC expression under non-permissive conditions.

00:07:30.417 --> 00:07:36.656
We then analyzed OTC activity; if it wasn't present, we would then Western blot

00:07:36.657 --> 00:07:44.297
the particular mutant after shift to 37 C and induction of OTC to see what the fate of the OTC subunit was.

00:07:44.298 --> 00:07:48.367
Was it present as a mature size protein, or was it still a precursor

00:07:48.368 --> 00:07:54.874
that hadn't been recognized by mitochondria and so our idea was to dissect the mitochondrial import pathway.

00:07:54.875 --> 00:08:01.881
Well, as we were asking this question about refolding inside the mitochondrial matrix,

00:08:01.882 --> 00:08:05.117
we knew what we would be looking for in the form of a mutant

00:08:05.118 --> 00:08:08.621
that affects refolding in the mitochondrial matrix space.

00:08:08.622 --> 00:08:13.926
We would have mature size OTC subunits because their signal peptide would be cleaved

00:08:13.927 --> 00:08:18.831
and they'd be inside the matrix space, but we would have no enzymatic activity,

00:08:18.832 --> 00:08:22.735
because they would have failed to correctly fold, and in the case of OTC,

00:08:22.736 --> 00:08:26.639
trimerize into the active form of the enzyme.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.377
So, we started to look through our bank of mutants

00:08:31.378 --> 00:08:35.748
and to our astonishment, almost right away, having asked this question,

00:08:35.749 --> 00:08:38.651
we found a mutant in which exactly that happened.

00:08:38.652 --> 00:08:45.658
So, OTC was our initial reporter enzyme and what we found was that OTC subunits entered,

00:08:45.659 --> 00:08:48.094
they reached their mature size,

00:08:48.095 --> 00:08:50.930
and then there was no biological activity at all.

00:08:50.931 --> 00:08:53.666
So, this astonished us.

00:08:53.667 --> 00:08:56.602
It seemed like heresy to have a result like this.

00:08:56.603 --> 00:09:01.841
So, we though, uh huh, we better start to look at endogenous yeast proteins

00:09:01.842 --> 00:09:04.076
and see what is happening to them.

00:09:04.077 --> 00:09:09.115
So, we looked at this particular protein, the F1 beta subunit of ATPase,

00:09:09.116 --> 00:09:11.017
of the F1 ATPase.

00:09:11.018 --> 00:09:17.823
And we found that in fact, similarly, this beta subunit had failed to assemble

00:09:17.824 --> 00:09:24.163
into this stalk like structure and the subunits themselves looked like had misfolded

00:09:24.164 --> 00:09:27.333
in the mitochondrial matrix space.

00:09:27.334 --> 00:09:33.172
We were in a bit of a quandary, because, again, it seemed like heresy,

00:09:33.173 --> 00:09:38.110
and we couldn't really feel comfortable about the data at hand

00:09:38.111 --> 00:09:42.381
and we were sort of pondering what proteins to look at next

00:09:42.382 --> 00:09:47.086
when thankfully, the phone rang and it was Ulrich Hartl and Walter Neupert

00:09:47.087 --> 00:09:52.758
calling from Munich to say, well, we understand that you guys have some mutants of yeast

00:09:52.759 --> 00:09:55.361
that affect mitochondrial protein import

00:09:55.362 --> 00:10:01.400
and we would love to get together with you and perhaps help on some of the biochemistry of those mutants,

00:10:01.401 --> 00:10:03.269
if you'd have some interest.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:07.607
So, at that point in history, my lab had four people in it,

00:10:07.608 --> 00:10:12.979
and we weren't very well versed at fractionating yeast mitochondria,

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:15.314
whereas these were world experts,

00:10:15.315 --> 00:10:20.987
so we were just delighted to interact and I went to Munich the next week

00:10:20.988 --> 00:10:25.424
and presented some of the better described mutants that we had

00:10:25.425 --> 00:10:29.295
that affected the mitochondrial signal peptide processing.

00:10:29.296 --> 00:10:33.466
But I also, at the end of my talk, described this particular mutant,

00:10:33.467 --> 00:10:39.272
and Walter and Ulrich were somewhat worried that what was happening in this mutant

00:10:39.273 --> 00:10:43.309
was that proteins were coming in through this contact site

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:47.079
but weren't getting entirely into the mitochondrial matrix,

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:52.185
that they were poking their N-terminal signal peptides in and the signals were being cleaved,

00:10:52.186 --> 00:10:54.654
but then the proteins weren't entirely entering

00:10:54.655 --> 00:10:58.457
and of course, they wouldn't fold if they couldn't enter through the membranes.

00:10:58.458 --> 00:11:02.962
And so, I said, well I'd be absolutely delighted to send you the mutant

00:11:02.963 --> 00:11:08.201
and you can make mitochondria, I am sure, from this mutant, very effectively,

00:11:08.202 --> 00:11:16.342
and analyze to see what's going on and in fact, as soon as I got back, I sent the mutant to Munich,

00:11:16.343 --> 00:11:22.181
and two weeks later, Ulrich called up to say, excitedly, you're right,

00:11:22.182 --> 00:11:27.587
the proteins are entirely in the mitochondrial matrix, I can add exogenous protease,

00:11:27.588 --> 00:11:32.358
and it doesn't touch them, so it seems like they have gone into the matrix space

00:11:32.359 --> 00:11:35.228
and they have failed to fold correctly.

00:11:35.229 --> 00:11:39.565
So, this was a really wonderful time where there was a lot of back and forth,

00:11:39.566 --> 00:11:47.273
with Ulrich and I crossing the Atlantic to visit each other and discuss the phenotype of this mutant.

00:11:47.274 --> 00:11:51.143
This is us together at his parents' house,

00:11:51.144 --> 00:11:55.481
here's his lovely wife Manajit, who is a biochemist herself

00:11:55.482 --> 00:11:59.485
and has worked some on the GroEL system as well, in recent times,

00:11:59.486 --> 00:12:02.088
a chaperonin of bacteria.

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:05.992
And maybe this is her telling us how this is all going to work out.

00:12:05.993 --> 00:12:13.499
But in any case, we carried out together a set of further experiments

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.402
on a number of mitochondrial matrix proteins

00:12:16.403 --> 00:12:18.604
to see what was happening to them.

00:12:18.605 --> 00:12:22.408
And one of the most exciting results came with this particular protein,

00:12:22.409 --> 00:12:24.977
the Rieske iron sulfur protein.

00:12:24.978 --> 00:12:30.816
So, this is a protein that is imported to the matrix space and has to be cleaved twice

00:12:30.817 --> 00:12:35.621
as a monomer, before it enters the inner mitochondrial membrane.

00:12:35.622 --> 00:12:38.658
And when we looked at this iron sulfur protein,

00:12:38.659 --> 00:12:42.828
we found it in either a completely uncleaved state

00:12:42.829 --> 00:12:47.033
or in a once-cleaved state, as if the protein had not folded correctly

00:12:47.034 --> 00:12:52.071
and had not undergone the proper cleavage that enables its correct biogenesis.

00:12:52.072 --> 00:12:58.110
So, this was the first of a monomeric protein where we saw effects in this particular mutant.

00:12:58.111 --> 00:13:04.383
We, at this point, we rescued the mutant with a yeast library.

00:13:04.384 --> 00:13:12.225
So, we put on a bank of yeast clones and the gene that rescued was sequenced

00:13:12.226 --> 00:13:17.196
by Ming Cheng during, I believe it was summer 1988.

00:13:17.197 --> 00:13:23.069
And at this point, we knew that there was a modestly heat inducible protein

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:27.874
inside mitochondria that had been identified the year before by Richard Hallberg,

00:13:27.875 --> 00:13:30.376
working in Iowa, in Tetrahymena.

00:13:30.377 --> 00:13:38.217
And we had a feeling that Hallberg was analyzing the sequence of the corresponding homologue in yeast

00:13:38.218 --> 00:13:41.554
with a view to doing further experiments in that system.

00:13:41.555 --> 00:13:48.427
So, on a Saturday afternoon in that summer, Ming Cheng called up Richard Hallberg

00:13:48.428 --> 00:13:54.634
with, to see whether her sequence of this gene would match the sequence that he had

00:13:54.635 --> 00:13:56.769
of the homologue he was studying.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.972
Well, I had gone to JFK airport to pick up Ulrich,

00:13:59.973 --> 00:14:03.910
because the two of us were going to a Gordon conference on mitochondria the next day,

00:14:03.911 --> 00:14:10.583
and my son came with us, it was many hours, but we got back to the, to our little beach house

00:14:10.584 --> 00:14:15.021
at around 6 o'clock in the evening and there's Ming Cheng and the rest of the lab,

00:14:15.022 --> 00:14:19.992
standing in our front yard and Ming's just jumping up and down holding a sequencing gel,

00:14:19.993 --> 00:14:25.231
saying, "it's a dead match, it's a dead match!" and we said, well, what's the dead match?

00:14:25.232 --> 00:14:31.337
She said, oh, I called up Hallberg, our protein corresponds to this ring assembly that he's studying!

00:14:31.338 --> 00:14:38.010
And so, two and two came together in a really amazing moment and we had a wonderful evening of celebration,

00:14:38.011 --> 00:14:44.183
but basically, what it amounted to was what we then called heat shock protein 60,

00:14:44.184 --> 00:14:48.888
collectively, with Richard and Ulrich and Walter and everybody.

00:14:48.889 --> 00:14:52.992
This modestly heat inducible protein

00:14:52.993 --> 00:14:59.565
was actually assisting the folding of all these proteins being imported in the mitochondrial matrix.

00:14:59.566 --> 00:15:06.239
And in fact, however, Hsp60 is essential at all temperatures,

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:12.011
so its assistance to polypeptide chain folding is required under normal conditions,

00:15:12.012 --> 00:15:15.815
which embody, often, relatively high temperatures,

00:15:15.816 --> 00:15:19.418
and very high solute concentrations in the mitochondrial matrix,

00:15:19.419 --> 00:15:22.889
ergo this machine is essential under all conditions.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:28.094
Now, shortly after this, Ulrich carried out a beautiful experiment with Joachim Ostermann

00:15:28.095 --> 00:15:33.466
where they analyzed another monomeric protein, dihydrofolate reductase.

00:15:33.467 --> 00:15:39.138
And what they could see was that when they put a signal peptide on DHFR to make it go into mitochondria,

00:15:39.139 --> 00:15:41.440
much as in the Schatz experiments,

00:15:41.441 --> 00:15:46.812
letting it go into mitochondria, it became associated with this complex,

00:15:46.813 --> 00:15:52.418
with this ring assembly that Hallberg had first identified, the Hsp60 complex,

00:15:52.419 --> 00:15:56.522
as long as ATP was relatively deprived from mitochondria.

00:15:56.523 --> 00:16:02.395
If they added ATP back to this system, they now saw DHFR depart from this complex,

00:16:02.396 --> 00:16:05.031
and appear as a native folded protein.

00:16:05.032 --> 00:16:09.368
While the protein, I should say, was associated with Hsp60,

00:16:09.369 --> 00:16:12.672
it seemed to be non-native, very protease susceptible,

00:16:12.673 --> 00:16:17.810
recognizable by antibodies that specifically saw non-native forms of DHFR,

00:16:17.811 --> 00:16:23.015
and so these experiments, with Rieske iron sulfur protein, with DHFR,

00:16:23.016 --> 00:16:28.688
convinced us that Hsp60 was some sort of a folding machine in the mitochondrial matrix.

00:16:28.689 --> 00:16:36.429
And of course, there's some generality to this because when we saw the sequence of Hsp60,

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:41.467
we knew already that it was going to be homologous, and is homologous,

00:16:41.468 --> 00:16:47.173
to the GroEL protein in the bacterial cytoplasm, essential in that context,

00:16:47.174 --> 00:16:50.776
and had been shown to be involved, or had been implicated

00:16:50.777 --> 00:16:53.980
early as being involved in lambda phage head assembly,

00:16:53.981 --> 00:16:58.518
now this idea had to move to lambda phage protein folding.

00:16:58.519 --> 00:17:04.090
There was also a protein inside of chloroplasts called the rubisco binding protein,

00:17:04.091 --> 00:17:08.528
identified by John Ellis as being required for assembly of rubisco,

00:17:08.529 --> 00:17:14.000
which is a CO2 fixing enzyme, in fact, the most abundant protein in the biosphere.

00:17:14.001 --> 00:17:19.205
This now, had to go to the level of thinking that rubisco binding protein

00:17:19.206 --> 00:17:23.943
was actually mediating protein folding of rubisco subunits.

00:17:23.944 --> 00:17:29.582
And finally, shortly thereafter, working together, Ulrich and I, with Jonathan Trent,

00:17:29.583 --> 00:17:34.053
identified a heat shot protein in thermophilic archaebacteria

00:17:34.054 --> 00:17:38.791
that we called thermophilic factor 55, another double ring assembly,

00:17:38.792 --> 00:17:43.396
that is highly induced when you take these normally heat loving bacteria

00:17:43.397 --> 00:17:45.698
and take them to an even higher temperature,

00:17:45.699 --> 00:17:51.704
that turns out to look just like a chaperonin, and it also could turn over ATP

00:17:51.705 --> 00:17:54.807
and behave just like these other ring assemblies.

00:17:54.808 --> 00:17:59.278
And, in fact, when we analyze this subunit,   we found a homology

00:17:59.279 --> 00:18:04.951
to a protein in the eukaryotic cytosol called TCP1, or CCT for short,

00:18:04.952 --> 00:18:12.191
this being a member of the eukaryotic cytosolic chaperonin 1 subunit of the 8 subunit rings

00:18:12.192 --> 00:18:18.030
that comprise that chaperonin, essential for folding actin, tubulin, beta propeller proteins,

00:18:18.031 --> 00:18:19.799
and many other proteins.

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:24.971
So, the generality of these ring assemblies in mediating protein folding to the native state

00:18:24.972 --> 00:18:27.540
seemed to be getting established.

00:18:27.541 --> 00:18:30.243
And so finally, mechanistically,

00:18:30.244 --> 00:18:35.748
20 years later, we understand at least how the bacterial chaperonin system works

00:18:35.749 --> 00:18:40.920
in pretty good detail now, with much biochemistry, X-ray crystallography,

00:18:40.921 --> 00:18:47.627
NMR studies and a lot of EM conducted on this particular machinery, the GroEL machine

00:18:47.628 --> 00:18:53.833
and its cooperating component, GroES, and the bottom line in terms of mechanism is

00:18:53.834 --> 00:19:00.039
that an open ring of GroEL with all this hydrophobic surface specifically binds non-native proteins

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:04.677
through their exposure of hydrophobic surface that will be buried to the interior

00:19:04.678 --> 00:19:09.982
in the native state, thus GroEL specifically recognizes non-native proteins

00:19:09.983 --> 00:19:15.721
and once it's bound to protein, when GroES now encapsulates the same ring

00:19:15.722 --> 00:19:22.562
that's bound by polypeptide, the polypeptide is released into this hydrophilic folding chamber,

00:19:22.563 --> 00:19:26.666
all this blue here, signifies electrostatic residues,

00:19:26.667 --> 00:19:31.537
and the polypeptide now folds in isolation, in a sequestered environment,

00:19:31.538 --> 00:19:38.444
where it simply can't aggregate with any other protein and it basically uses Anfinson's principles,

00:19:38.445 --> 00:19:46.619
its primary structure, to find its way to the native state without the fear of aggregation or going off-pathway

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:51.757
and being lost. So, in general, molecular chaperones,

00:19:51.758 --> 00:19:55.895
if one wants to talk about all of the classes of these components,

00:19:55.896 --> 00:20:01.601
they basically recognize exposed hydrophobic surfaces

00:20:01.602 --> 00:20:06.405
that are proffered when a protein misfolds, or during biogenesis,

00:20:06.406 --> 00:20:12.111
and the idea of the chaperone is to bind these surfaces and prevent them

00:20:12.112 --> 00:20:16.449
from self-associating with each other in multi-molecular aggregates.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:20.653
The idea of all of the chaperones is to prevent aggregation

00:20:20.654 --> 00:20:24.190
by binding these surfaces in various geometric contexts.

00:20:24.191 --> 00:20:27.026
The remarkable thing about the chaperonins, however,

00:20:27.027 --> 00:20:32.465
that's really unique, is that they use nucleotide to release the polypeptide

00:20:32.466 --> 00:20:38.304
into a sequestered folding chamber where the polypeptide really has no chance of aggregation

00:20:38.305 --> 00:20:42.408
and has a chance to fold using its own primary structure

00:20:42.409 --> 00:20:46.779
to the native state, one of nature's really most beautiful machines,

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:49.815
used for this final step of information transfer.

00:20:49.816 --> 00:20:54.220
So, thanks very much for listening to this lecture, I hope you enjoyed it.

00:20:54.221 --> 00:20:55.422
Thank you.

